1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,340
Have you ever wondered what the true nature is of the attachments you cling to so tightly, or the realities you take for granted?

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:15,120
Today, I will guide you all to deeply realize one of the profoundest wisdoms in Buddhism—the emptiness of all dharmas.

3
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:25,080
This is not only the truth proclaimed by the Buddha himself over two thousand five hundred years ago but also a beacon of wisdom that transcends time and space, illuminating our modern lives directly.

4
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:32,340
The empty nature of all phenomena, though seemingly simple as four characters on the surface, contains the key to unlocking the truth of life.

5
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,680
It does not tell us that everything is nonexistent nihilism, but rather reveals an even more astounding truth.

6
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:48,100
All that we cling to—material things, emotions, thoughts, and even our very sense of self—are not as solid, unchanging, or independently existing as we imagine them to be.

7
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:55,060
When you truly understand the meaning of emptiness, you will realize that it does not take anything away, but rather grants infinite possibilities.

8
00:00:56,140 --> 00:01:01,060
This is a wisdom of the Middle Way that transcends existence and non-existence, birth and death, gain and loss.

9
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:07,180
Dear friend with whom you share affinity, it is no coincidence that you are seeing this video and forming a connection with this wisdom at this moment.

10
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:14,560
If this Buddhist wisdom brings you joy in the Dharma, please write in the comments that it is indeed Shakyamuni Buddha.

11
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:18,640
This is both a blessing and an acknowledgment of the Buddha's wisdom.

12
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:22,680
The concept of the emptiness of all dharmas does not arise from nowhere.

13
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:25,060
It originates from the Buddha's realization under the Bodhi tree.

14
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:30,980
It originates from the serene night when the Buddha attained enlightenment under the Bodhi tree, revealing profound truths about the nature of the world to humanity.

15
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:38,080
Among the Buddha's teachings, the most direct scripture elucidating Śūnyatā (Emptiness) is the Heart Sutra (Prajñāpāramitā Hṛdaya).

16
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:53,080
In the sutra, there is a widely known passage: "Form is not different from emptiness; emptiness is not different from form. Form is emptiness; emptiness is form." These sixteen brief characters embody the crystallized wisdom spanning millennia, guiding countless practitioners toward the gate of liberation.

17
00:01:53,360 --> 00:02:00,240
It is said that during the time of the Buddha, there was a disciple named Kātyāyana who asked the Buddha about the issue of existence and non-existence.

18
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:06,440
People often fall into dualistic thinking, believing that the world either exists or does not exist.

19
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:12,480
The Buddha told Kāṇāyanī: If a person sees existence, they are on one side; if they see non-existence, they are on the other side.

20
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:19,920
The Tathagata's teaching dwells in the Middle Way; this is the origin of the renowned Madhyamaka philosophy and the key to understanding emptiness.

21
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:26,860
As the Dharma spread, the concept of emptiness gained richer interpretations across different periods and regions.

22
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:32,720
In India, Bodhisattva Nagarjuna expounded the concept of emptiness through the Madhyamaka (Middle Way) philosophy and dependent origination.

23
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:48,020
When Buddhism was transmitted eastward to China, the Sixth Patriarch Huineng’s "originally there is not a single thing; where can dust be attracted?" integrated the wisdom of emptiness into the sudden enlightenment method of Chan (Zen). In contrast, Tibetan Buddhism, through the Madhyamaka philosophy, elevated the cultivation of emptiness to a new height.

24
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:53,020
There is an ancient Zen koan that can help us understand the meaning of emptiness.

25
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:57,020
A monk asked Zen Master Fayan, "What is the true Dharma realm?"

26
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:10,600
The Dharma Eye responds, "Ten thousand miles become rice, within the village it becomes water." This seemingly plain and unremarkable answer directly points to the suchness of things. Things are just as they are, neither increasing nor decreasing, with no fixed, unchanging essence to be grasped or clung to.

27
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:29,280
It is noteworthy that although the concept of emptiness has been expressed in various forms throughout the historical development of Buddhism, its core essence remains consistent: all phenomena lack inherent, independent, and permanent existence. This understanding dispels delusions, dissolves attachment to the phenomenal world, and guides sentient beings toward liberation.

28
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:42,040
When we stand in the 21st century and look back at this ancient wisdom, we find that it has not faded with the passage of time; on the contrary, under the illumination of modern science and philosophy, it shines with even greater brilliance.

29
00:03:42,360 --> 00:04:00,360
The quantum theory of modern physics, molecular biology's exploration of the essence of life, and psychology's study of the self-concept all corroborate the Buddha's teachings from different perspectives: there is no independent, permanent, and unchanging entity; all phenomena arise, exist, and cease through mutual dependence and interaction.

30
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:10,460
This ancient and eternal wisdom transcends cultural and temporal boundaries, becoming a crucial key to understanding the human condition and seeking inner peace.

31
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:18,300
Now, let us delve deeper into the core essence of the emptiness of all dharmas, unveiling the mysterious veil of this treasure of wisdom.

32
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:24,000
To understand the emptiness (Śūnyatā) of all dharmas, we must first comprehend the true meaning of these four characters.

33
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:36,640
All dharmas refer to all phenomena and things in the world, encompassing not only the physical world that we can see and touch but also our thoughts, emotions, memories, as well as more abstract concepts and ideas.

34
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:46,000
From the microscopic atoms and molecules to the macroscopic galaxies and cosmos, from the existence of matter to the activities of mind, all fall within the scope of dharmas.

35
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:51,400
The emptiness within emptiness is one of the most easily misunderstood concepts in Buddhism.

36
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:55,140
Many people mistakenly believe that emptiness means nothingness or non-existence.

37
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,420
This is a serious misrepresentation of the Dharma.

38
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:03,820
The true meaning of emptiness is the lack of inherent self-nature, that is, the absence of an independent, permanent, and unchanging essence.

39
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:07,200
"Form" refers to the outward appearance or characteristics of things.

40
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:14,340
Therefore, the emptiness of all dharmas teaches us that behind the appearances of all things, there is a lack of an independent and unchanging essence.

41
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:17,020
Let me explain with a simple example.

42
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:24,220
Imagine the smartphone in your hand; on the surface, it appears to be an independently existing physical object.

43
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:30,060
However, upon deeper reflection, we realize that the existence of this mobile phone depends on countless conditions.

44
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:37,400
Metal, glass, plastic and other material substances, the design by engineers, the production by factories, as well as the support of the power system.

45
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:42,740
If you disassemble this mobile phone, you will find that it is composed of thousands of individual components.

46
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,180
And each component is composed of even more fundamental elements.

47
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,760
These elements are in turn composed of atoms.

48
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:51,840
Atoms also contain protons, neutrons, and electrons.

49
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:56,360
Ultimately, you will find that there is nothing that can be called a truly existent essence.

50
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:00,860
This mobile phone is merely the product of countless causes and conditions temporarily coming together.

51
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:03,380
It itself does not possess an inherent, unchanging nature.

52
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:06,580
The same principle applies to ourselves as well.

53
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,060
We habitually believe in a fixed and unchanging self.

54
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:17,880
However, the Dharma teaches us that this self is merely a temporary assemblage of the five aggregates: form (rūpa), feeling (vedanā), perception (saññā), mental formations (saṅkhāra), and consciousness (viññāṇa).

55
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:22,520
Our bodies are in a constant state of metabolism, with cells continuously dying and being reborn.

56
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:27,120
Our emotions and thoughts are constantly changing in accordance with the environment and conditions.

57
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:32,400
Upon deep observation, you will find that there is no permanent and unchanging self.

58
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:36,340
This is the wisdom of no-self, which is the key to understanding emptiness.

59
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:47,060
However, it is important to note that "no-self" does not mean the self does not exist; rather, it means that the self is not an independent, fixed, and unchanging entity, but a composite temporarily manifested dependent on various conditions.

60
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:53,680
In Buddhism, there is an important concept called dependent origination (pratītyasamutpāda), which means that all phenomena arise due to the convergence of causes and conditions.

61
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:54,300
Dependent origination arises from the coming together of causes and conditions.

62
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:57,080
Dependent origination and emptiness of inherent existence are both fundamental truths.

63
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:01,560
Precisely because all phenomena arise dependently, they are all empty of inherent existence.

64
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:04,940
Precisely because they are empty in nature, they can arise dependent on causes and conditions.

65
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,160
This is not a contradiction, but a principle of two sides of one entity.

66
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:16,480
Imagine a river; Heraclitus said that one cannot step into the same river twice, for the waters are constantly flowing and changing.

67
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:25,560
A river appears to be a continuous and enduring entity, but in reality, it is changing every moment, with no fixed and unchanging essence of the river.

68
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:33,020
It is precisely because the river lacks a fixed and unchanging essence—emptiness—that it can continuously flow and change, arising dependently.

69
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:39,600
Precisely because it is in constant flow and change, arising dependent on causes and conditions, it has no fixed, unchanging essence—emptiness.

70
00:07:39,860 --> 00:07:44,460
After understanding the meaning of emptiness, we need to clarify that emptiness is not nihilism.

71
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:54,900
The emptiness spoken of in Buddhism does not mean that things do not exist; rather, it means that things do not exist in the way we imagine them to. They lack an independent, permanent, and unchanging essence.

72
00:07:55,140 --> 00:08:01,420
This understanding breaks our stubborn perception of the world and opens up a completely new perspective for viewing it.

73
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:11,000
But I must also tell you a deeper truth, which is a trap that many Buddhist practitioners easily fall into: attachment to emptiness itself is also a form of attachment.

74
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:28,020
The Buddha said in the Diamond Sutra, if a bodhisattva has the notions of self, person, sentient being, or life span, they are not truly a bodhisattva. Similarly, if we cling to the concept of emptiness, regarding emptiness as some kind of entity or absolute truth, then we fall into another form of attachment.

75
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:35,500
True wisdom is neither attached to existence nor to non-existence; it transcends duality and returns to the Middle Way.

76
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:43,340
This leads to a seemingly paradoxical yet profound statement: Form is emptiness; emptiness is form—all phenomena in the world.

77
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:49,300
The essence of form is emptiness, but emptiness does not exist independently apart from all phenomena in the world.

78
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:54,620
Emptiness manifests through all phenomena in the world, and all phenomena in the world exist through emptiness.

79
00:08:54,920 --> 00:09:02,380
This is a wisdom that transcends language and concepts, attainable only through deep contemplation and meditation.

80
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,620
The wisdom of emptiness is also manifested in a profound understanding of impermanence.

81
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:16,860
Impermanence is one of the Three Marks of Existence in Buddhism. It refers to all conditioned phenomena, which arise due to causes and conditions and are in a constant state of flux, lacking any eternal and unchanging essence.

82
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:23,660
From the microscopic perspective, science tells us that within the atoms composing matter, electrons are in constant motion.

83
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:28,680
From a macroscopic perspective, planets rotate on their axes and revolve around stars, while the universe is expanding.

84
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:32,860
From the perspective of human society, dynasties rise and fall, and civilizations flourish and decline.

85
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:39,340
From the perspective of individual life, we undergo birth, aging, illness, and death, as well as emotional fluctuations and changes in thoughts.

86
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:44,720
The ever-present change teaches us that grasping anything is like trying to hold flowing water.

87
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:49,380
I often use the ocean and its waves to illustrate the relationship between emptiness and phenomena.

88
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:54,100
Waves appear to be independent entities, some high and some low, some large and some small.

89
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:57,720
But essentially, they are all temporary forms of seawater.

90
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:02,120
Waves depend on the seawater for their existence; without the seawater, there are no waves.

91
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:06,080
At the same time, the seawater manifests itself through the form of waves.

92
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:11,420
Although the waves continuously arise and cease, the true nature of the ocean water remains unchanging.

93
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:18,260
Similarly, although all phenomena in the world continuously undergo change, arising and ceasing, their essential nature of emptiness remains unchanging.

94
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:25,720
The wisdom of emptiness is not only present in Mahayana Buddhism but also has its unique expressions in various schools.

95
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:34,440
Zen Buddhism describes the threefold realm of "seeing the mountain as a mountain, seeing the mountain not as a mountain, and seeing the mountain again as a mountain," which portrays the understanding and realization of emptiness (śūnyatā).

96
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:47,300
In Pure Land Buddhism, whether one recites the Buddha’s name once or ten times, all can attain rebirth in the Pure Land, reflecting the transcendence of emptiness over the concepts of time and quantity. In Vajrayana Buddhism, the attainment of Buddhahood in one’s current body is an innovative application of the wisdom of emptiness in practice methods.

97
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:55,040
The concept of "Three Thousand Realms in a Single Thought" proposed by Master Zhiyi, the founder of the Tiantai School, is a theory of emptiness and a profound exposition of the wisdom of emptiness.

98
00:10:55,240 --> 00:11:02,800
He pointed out that a single thought encompasses the Three Thousand Worlds, the vast within the minute, and eternity within a moment.

99
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:09,040
This is not an exaggerated mystical expression, but a profound insight into the unity of mind, where there is no duality between inside and outside.

100
00:11:09,320 --> 00:11:19,400
In the Mahāprajñāpāramitāśāstra, Bodhisattva Nāgārjuna explains emptiness as follows: If phenomena truly existed, they would be real; consequently, they could be destroyed. If there were no phenomena at all, this would imply permanence.

101
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:26,840
Because of the nature of emptiness, this passage reveals how emptiness transcends the two extreme views of annihilation and eternalism.

102
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:32,960
If one believes that things possess an inherent essence, then when they disappear, one falls into the view of annihilation.

103
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:38,040
If one believes that things inherently do not exist, one will fall into the error of the eternalism and nihilism view.

104
00:11:38,460 --> 00:11:45,000
The middle way wisdom of emptiness neither falls into existence nor into non-existence, transcending these dualistic concepts.

105
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:50,080
It is important to note that understanding emptiness requires caution against two common misconceptions.

106
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,180
The first misconception is to understand emptiness as nihilism, meaning that nothing exists at all.

107
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:57,640
This is a serious misinterpretation of the Dharma.

108
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:03,480
The Buddha clearly pointed out that one should neither cling to existence nor cling to non-existence.

109
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:12,100
In the Madhyamaka, it is said that one should not say "existence," nor "non-existence," nor "both existence and non-existence," nor "neither existence nor non-existence." To express the entirety, one speaks of "existence."

110
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:20,920
This passage tells us that emptiness transcends the realm of language and concepts; any attempt to fix it with conventional language will lead to obsession.

111
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:27,000
The second misconception is to understand emptiness as a kind of metaphysical entity or essence.

112
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:38,220
Some people, upon hearing about the emptiness of all phenomena, imagine that there is something called "emptiness" existing either outside or inside the phenomenal world. However, this precisely contradicts the true meaning of emptiness.

113
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:45,140
Emptiness is not an entity, but a description of the essential nature of phenomena, indicating the absence of an independent, permanent self-nature.

114
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:53,520
In the practice journey, we may encounter the challenge that the ability to understand emptiness is a very ambiguous principle, yet it is difficult to truly realize and apply.

115
00:12:53,760 --> 00:13:04,860
There exists a vast gulf between intellectual understanding and actual realization, much like a person who has read countless books about swimming but has never entered the water.

116
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:11,140
True wisdom of emptiness must be experienced and verified through continuous meditation and practical living.

117
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:16,140
A groundbreaking insight may help you gain a deeper understanding of emptiness.

118
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:21,700
Emptiness is not only a description of the phenomenal world but also a reflection on our mode of cognition.

119
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:28,860
We are accustomed to understanding the world through concepts and language, slicing the flowing reality into fixed notions.

120
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:35,480
However, concepts and language themselves are human constructs and cannot fully capture the entirety of reality.

121
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:43,620
The wisdom of emptiness reminds us not to be imprisoned by our own concepts, and to constantly be aware of the gap between concepts and ultimate reality.

122
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:50,580
When we truly realize emptiness, we find that it is not a passive negation but an active liberation.

123
00:13:50,860 --> 00:14:00,100
It liberates us from attachment to fixed identities, roles, and concepts, allowing us to face life with greater openness and flexibility.

124
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:08,100
From the perspective of emptiness, failure is no longer the end, and suffering is no longer eternal; all experiences are processes of continuous flux and change.

125
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:13,660
We can begin anew in every moment, having understood the profound meaning of the emptiness of all dharmas.

126
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:14,460
We can begin anew in each present moment, having understood the profound meaning of the emptiness of all dharmas.

127
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:17,780
How can we apply this wisdom to our daily lives?

128
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:23,100
Firstly, the wisdom of emptiness can help us face change and impermanence.

129
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:31,660
When we deeply understand that everything is changing and nothing is eternal and unchanging, we can more calmly accept the ups and downs in life.

130
00:14:32,020 --> 00:14:39,360
Losing cherished possessions, experiencing changes in relationships, and facing the decline of health no longer cause such profound suffering.

131
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:45,860
We know that attachment is the root of suffering, and only by letting go of attachment can one attain inner peace.

132
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:51,220
Secondly, the wisdom of emptiness can help us reduce attachment to the self.

133
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:58,760
We are often bound by the concepts of "I" and "mine"—my achievements, my failures, my reputation, my status.

134
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:09,460
When we realize that the self is not a fixed, unchanging entity but a constantly changing process, we can alleviate the burden of self-centeredness and live more effortlessly and freely.

135
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:14,760
Third, the wisdom of emptiness can help us cultivate compassion.

136
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:25,420
When we realize that all sentient beings are interdependent, without absolute boundaries or distinctions, we can transcend the duality of self and others, and regard the welfare of others as our own responsibility.

137
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:32,360
True compassion is not a condescending act of giving, but a natural response to the intrinsic interdependence of all life.

138
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:42,740
Fourth, the wisdom of emptiness helps us maintain a middle way approach to life, avoiding both extreme hedonism and extreme asceticism.

139
00:15:43,180 --> 00:15:50,060
Neither blindly optimistic nor pessimistically negative, one can actively engage in worldly affairs while maintaining inner detachment.

140
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:55,540
This balanced attitude toward life is the practical manifestation of the wisdom of emptiness.

141
00:15:55,820 --> 00:16:02,340
Ultimately, the wisdom of emptiness can help us cultivate mindfulness and awareness in our daily lives.

142
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:14,560
When we focus on washing dishes while washing dishes, focus on walking while walking, without being pulled by past memories or future plans, we are experiencing the emptiness of the present moment in our own way.

143
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:22,700
There is no fixed and unchanging self experiencing, nor is there a fixed and unchanging object being experienced; there is only the pure flow of experience itself.

144
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:28,480
As Bodhisattva Nagarjuna states in the "Mūlamadhyamakakārikā," all phenomena arise because of emptiness understood as the emptiness of inherent existence.

145
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:31,100
If there is no emptiness, nothing can be accomplished. The phrase should be revised to: If there is no emptiness-nature, nothing can be accomplished.

146
00:16:31,140 --> 00:16:35,780
It is precisely because of emptiness that all phenomena in the world arise, change, and cease.

147
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:41,460
It is precisely because of emptiness that we can transcend attachment and attain true freedom and wisdom.

148
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:50,700
Attachment to the emptiness of all dharmas is not merely an abstract philosophical theory, but also an art of living—a completely new perspective on viewing the world.

149
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:57,840
It teaches us not to be deceived by appearances, not to be constrained by concepts, and not to be troubled by attachments.

150
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:06,700
When we let go of attachments and transcend dualistic oppositions, we can experience the intrinsic nature of life—tranquility, harmony, and freedom.

151
00:17:07,119 --> 00:17:15,099
In this rapidly changing era, in this world full of uncertainty, the wisdom of the emptiness and suchness of all dharmas is especially precious.

152
00:17:15,260 --> 00:17:24,040
It helps us face change, accept impermanence, maintain inner peace amidst chaos, and find meaning in the midst of turmoil.

153
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:32,620
If today's sharing has inspired you, I hope you can bring this wisdom into your life, sincerely contemplate it, and put it into practice through your actions.

154
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:40,240
You are also welcome to share your insights and experiences in the comments below, so that we can grow together in the wisdom of the Dharma.

